  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Let $f$ and $g$ be two functions such that $f(3x)=2x$ and $g(x-2)=3x+3$. Find the rule for the function $f(g(x))$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$f(g(x))=2x+6$.\n\nRecover $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{3}$ and $g(x)=3x+9$, then substitute $g(x)$ into $f$.",
      "worked": "Let $a=3x$, so $x=\\frac{a}{3}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(a)&=2\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)\\\\f(a)&=\\frac{2a}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{3}$.\n\nLet $b=x-2$, so $x=b+2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}g(b)&=3(b+2)+3\\\\g(b)&=3b+9\\end{align*}$$\nThus $g(x)=3x+9$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=f(3x+9)\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{2(3x+9)}{3}\\\\f(g(x))&=2(x+3)\\\\f(g(x))&=2x+6\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
